1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,371
Here is a Cloud Run job
called translation-audio.

2
00:00:03,691 --> 00:00:08,277
It uses the same image as a translation
web app that works perfectly.

3
00:00:08,597 --> 00:00:11,051
We press Execute, and the job starts.

4
00:00:11,733 --> 00:00:12,733
Minutes pass.

5
00:00:13,034 --> 00:00:17,365
Nothing lands in the output bucket,
and the job never says it is done.

6
00:00:17,685 --> 00:00:20,586
It runs straight
into the task timeout we gave it.

7
00:00:20,906 --> 00:00:23,232
5 minutes, and the platform stops it.

8
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:28,736
The clue is the last line of the Dockerfile:
its default command starts the web server.

9
00:00:29,056 --> 00:00:32,792
The README's troubleshooting table even
lists the symptom:

10
00:00:32,792 --> 00:00:35,883
a job that starts a server
instead of finishing.

11
00:00:36,117 --> 00:00:37,802
Same image, same code.

12
00:00:38,122 --> 00:00:41,386
So why does the web app work,
while the job never ends?

13
00:00:41,706 --> 00:00:42,986
Hold that question.

14
00:00:44,817 --> 00:00:48,081
For the quiz,
this lecture comes down to five ideas.

15
00:00:48,401 --> 00:00:49,745
Service or job.

16
00:00:50,065 --> 00:00:51,729
Who pays for idle time.

17
00:00:52,049 --> 00:00:53,756
What you hand over to deploy.

18
00:00:54,076 --> 00:00:56,508
Who may call your code, and who it runs as.

19
00:00:56,828 --> 00:00:58,180
And the model endpoint:

20
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:01,414
where preprocessing lives,
and which function it calls.

21
00:01:01,650 --> 00:01:06,109
The gray tag, top right, names
the lecture slide behind each idea.

22
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:14,973
The lecture says Cloud Run runs a container
as a service or a job.

23
00:01:15,293 --> 00:01:20,114
The demo app shows both: one image,
two startup commands, serve or batch.

24
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:23,047
Serve starts gunicorn, a web server,

25
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:26,643
on the port Cloud Run gives it,
8080 by default.

26
00:01:26,963 --> 00:01:30,057
Then it waits,
and answers each request that arrives.

27
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:32,165
That shape is a service.

28
00:01:32,485 --> 00:01:36,032
It has a URL,
adds instances when traffic rises,

29
00:01:36,032 --> 00:01:38,693
and can scale to zero when it stops.

30
00:01:39,013 --> 00:01:42,362
It never finishes on purpose:
listening is its job.

31
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,093
Batch has a different shape.

32
00:01:44,413 --> 00:01:45,829
It reads the passage,

33
00:01:45,829 --> 00:01:49,875
translates it, generates
speech, saves the files, and exits.

34
00:01:50,195 --> 00:01:52,924
If a step fails, it exits with status 1,

35
00:01:52,924 --> 00:01:56,403
so the execution shows failed,
not a false success.

36
00:01:56,650 --> 00:02:01,450
The README calls this a job:
no URL, nothing waiting for requests.

37
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:04,501
Something starts it,
and it runs to completion.

38
00:02:04,750 --> 00:02:05,753
Now the hook.

39
00:02:06,073 --> 00:02:09,550
The job inherited
the image's default command, serve,

40
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:13,945
so it started a web server
and waited for requests that never come.

41
00:02:14,265 --> 00:02:17,401
The fix: the command python,
with the batch arguments.

42
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:20,067
Same image, and now the strip ends.

43
00:02:20,317 --> 00:02:21,554
Who presses start?

44
00:02:21,874 --> 00:02:24,050
In the README, you click Execute.

45
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:27,940
For a nightly run,
the usual trigger is Cloud Scheduler,

46
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:29,917
which this README does not use.

47
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:31,617
Try one.

48
00:02:31,897 --> 00:02:35,780
A container indexes new documents and exits,
once an hour.

49
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:38,595
Read the lifecycle, not the product names.

50
00:02:49,933 --> 00:02:52,942
B, a job on a Cloud Scheduler timetable:

51
00:02:52,942 --> 00:02:56,930
it runs to completion and costs
nothing between runs.

52
00:02:57,250 --> 00:02:59,441
A pays for an instance all hour,

53
00:02:59,441 --> 00:03:03,480
and an idle service's CPU is throttled,
so the loop stalls.

54
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:07,960
C breaks the service contract:
an instance that exits has crashed.

55
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:11,928
D works, but the VM is paid for all hour
while it idles.

56
00:03:12,248 --> 00:03:13,891
Why that matters is next.

57
00:03:14,133 --> 00:03:16,894
So the rule: if the question says it runs,

58
00:03:16,894 --> 00:03:21,429
writes its output and exits,
or that it runs every night, pick a job.

59
00:03:21,749 --> 00:03:27,104
If it says it answers requests, has a URL,
or listens on a port, pick a service.

60
00:03:32,383 --> 00:03:35,562
The lecture promises you pay only
while your code runs.

61
00:03:35,882 --> 00:03:38,698
Let's make that exact: two meters, one day.

62
00:03:39,018 --> 00:03:40,767
The yellow ticks are requests.

63
00:03:41,016 --> 00:03:43,128
The top row is a virtual machine.

64
00:03:43,448 --> 00:03:47,032
Its meter runs from boot to shutdown,
requests or not.

65
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:50,894
The gold shading is idle time,
and you pay for all of it.

66
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:54,745
The bottom row is a function,
or a Cloud Run service

67
00:03:54,745 --> 00:03:59,538
with request-based billing
and minimum instances 0, as in the README.

68
00:03:59,858 --> 00:04:04,040
Its meter moves only
with invocations and how long each one runs.

69
00:04:04,450 --> 00:04:07,138
Now stretch the quiet gaps between requests.

70
00:04:07,458 --> 00:04:08,766
The VM meter grows,

71
00:04:08,766 --> 00:04:12,002
because idle hours
are still provisioned hours.

72
00:04:12,322 --> 00:04:16,482
The function meter does not move at all:
no requests, no charge.

73
00:04:16,716 --> 00:04:21,879
Zero idle cost has a price,
and slide 16 names it: the cold start.

74
00:04:22,199 --> 00:04:24,839
After a quiet stretch,
the first request waits

75
00:04:24,839 --> 00:04:26,273
while an instance starts.

76
00:04:26,593 --> 00:04:29,367
The README's remedy is minimum instances.

77
00:04:29,687 --> 00:04:33,753
Set it to 1, and a warm instance
stays on for the first request,

78
00:04:33,753 --> 00:04:35,468
with its own meter running.

79
00:04:35,716 --> 00:04:38,959
That always-on instance
now sits in the VM row.

80
00:04:39,279 --> 00:04:42,258
So does next lecture's Vertex AI endpoint:

81
00:04:42,258 --> 00:04:46,087
its deploy call asks
for a minimum replica count of 1,

82
00:04:46,087 --> 00:04:49,562
which the README calls the part
that costs money.

83
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:51,293
Test it on a new case.

84
00:04:51,613 --> 00:04:55,453
Twice as many requests,
each just as long, in the same days.

85
00:04:55,773 --> 00:04:57,160
Which bill changes?

86
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,600
B, only the function's.

87
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:14,133
Watch: every request splits in two,
and the function meter climbs,

88
00:05:14,133 --> 00:05:17,643
since every invocation
and its running time are billed.

89
00:05:17,963 --> 00:05:22,281
The VM meter does not move,
because it bills provisioned hours:

90
00:05:22,281 --> 00:05:24,405
A is backwards, and C is wrong.

91
00:05:24,725 --> 00:05:27,861
D confuses no minimum charge with free work.

92
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,511
So the rule is not, serverless is cheap.

93
00:05:30,831 --> 00:05:33,284
The rule is: is anything always on?

94
00:05:33,604 --> 00:05:37,124
Idle time moves only
the bills of always-on things.

95
00:05:37,444 --> 00:05:41,241
Request count and duration
move only the pay-per-use bill.

96
00:05:45,983 --> 00:05:47,732
Next, what you hand over.

97
00:05:48,052 --> 00:05:51,426
The lecture deploys one translate
app four ways:

98
00:05:51,426 --> 00:05:55,220
virtual machine or serverless,
code file or container.

99
00:05:55,540 --> 00:05:57,951
Earlier lectures filled the VM row.

100
00:05:58,271 --> 00:06:00,276
Today fills the serverless row.

101
00:06:00,516 --> 00:06:05,337
For a function you hand over a source file,
its requirements file, and one name.

102
00:06:05,657 --> 00:06:10,553
The file is main.py:
an HTTP handler called hello_http,

103
00:06:10,553 --> 00:06:15,983
which reads the text
and two language codes from the request.

104
00:06:16,233 --> 00:06:17,899
That name is the entry point:

105
00:06:17,899 --> 00:06:20,542
the Python function
the framework should call.

106
00:06:20,862 --> 00:06:23,486
Not the file name, and not the service name.

107
00:06:23,733 --> 00:06:25,034
Then Google builds.

108
00:06:25,354 --> 00:06:30,345
Cloud Build installs the requirements,
configures the Functions Framework,

109
00:06:30,345 --> 00:06:33,312
and publishes an image to Artifact Registry.

110
00:06:33,632 --> 00:06:37,216
This happens once, at build time,
not on every request.

111
00:06:37,450 --> 00:06:39,989
The framework finds your function
by that name.

112
00:06:40,309 --> 00:06:44,383
Type main as the entry point,
and there is nothing called main to find.

113
00:06:44,703 --> 00:06:48,159
The function build fails,
and its build logs tell you why.

114
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:52,624
Slide 11 says a function runs
without managing containers.

115
00:06:52,944 --> 00:06:55,973
You do not manage one,
but Google still builds one.

116
00:06:56,293 --> 00:06:59,984
And the slides' Cloud Functions
are now Cloud Run functions.

117
00:07:00,233 --> 00:07:04,798
For a Cloud Run service,
you hand over a finished image by its URL.

118
00:07:05,118 --> 00:07:08,852
Deploying it builds nothing:
the image was built beforehand.

119
00:07:09,172 --> 00:07:11,642
Its Dockerfile picks the base Python,

120
00:07:11,642 --> 00:07:14,846
the default port, 8080,
and the startup command.

121
00:07:15,166 --> 00:07:16,660
You control all three.

122
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:19,844
Both are called through an HTTPS URL.

123
00:07:20,164 --> 00:07:24,516
The function takes new inputs
through its query string, with no redeploy.

124
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:30,323
Both end as the same thing:
a container instance on Cloud Run.

125
00:07:30,643 --> 00:07:33,331
A function is a container you did not write.

126
00:07:33,651 --> 00:07:36,873
On the quiz,
source plus an entry point means a function.

127
00:07:37,193 --> 00:07:40,904
Your own image, or your own startup command,
means a service.

128
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:49,244
Every deployed service
faces two permission questions,

129
00:07:49,244 --> 00:07:51,249
and the README keeps them separate.

130
00:07:51,569 --> 00:07:53,126
Who may call our public app?

131
00:07:53,446 --> 00:07:55,387
And what may the running app access?

132
00:07:55,633 --> 00:07:57,254
The left door is the caller.

133
00:07:57,574 --> 00:08:01,308
With public access allowed,
any browser's request gets in.

134
00:08:01,628 --> 00:08:06,034
Without it, a caller with no permission
is turned away with a 403,

135
00:08:06,034 --> 00:08:07,836
before your code even runs.

136
00:08:08,083 --> 00:08:10,920
The right door is who your code
is when it calls out.

137
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:16,002
The web app wears a service account badge,
translation-demo-runtime,

138
00:08:16,002 --> 00:08:17,069
with two roles:

139
00:08:17,069 --> 00:08:20,414
Agent Platform User
and Service Usage Consumer.

140
00:08:20,734 --> 00:08:22,910
You do not create or download a key.

141
00:08:23,150 --> 00:08:25,838
The code just asks for default credentials,

142
00:08:25,838 --> 00:08:30,339
and on Cloud Run the platform hands it
a token for the attached account.

143
00:08:30,659 --> 00:08:32,217
No key file anywhere.

144
00:08:32,467 --> 00:08:34,003
One more arrow, from you.

145
00:08:34,323 --> 00:08:36,491
To attach that badge to a service,

146
00:08:36,491 --> 00:08:39,742
your own identity needs
Service Account User on it.

147
00:08:40,062 --> 00:08:44,413
That is a deployment permission,
separate from what the badge itself may do.

148
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:46,442
The job gets its own badge.

149
00:08:46,762 --> 00:08:49,503
It has the same two Agent Platform roles,

150
00:08:49,503 --> 00:08:52,577
plus Storage Object Viewer
on the input bucket

151
00:08:52,577 --> 00:08:55,786
and Storage Object Creator
on the output bucket.

152
00:08:56,106 --> 00:08:58,757
The web app's account needs no bucket roles,

153
00:08:58,757 --> 00:09:01,589
because its audio goes
straight to the browser.

154
00:09:01,909 --> 00:09:03,487
One badge per workload.

155
00:09:03,733 --> 00:09:07,360
Slide 16 warns
that serverless is harder to debug.

156
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:12,305
The README says where to look:
build logs when a function build fails,

157
00:09:12,305 --> 00:09:16,533
service logs when a request fails,
and the job's execution logs.

158
00:09:16,853 --> 00:09:19,243
A finished build is not proof that it works.

159
00:09:19,483 --> 00:09:22,392
The rule:
public access answers who may call,

160
00:09:22,392 --> 00:09:25,883
and a refused caller is stopped
before your code runs.

161
00:09:26,203 --> 00:09:29,190
The service account answers
what your code may touch.

162
00:09:29,510 --> 00:09:33,393
When your own code is denied,
fix the badge's roles, not the caller.

163
00:09:38,433 --> 00:09:42,945
To serve a trained model,
the next lecture uses a Vertex AI endpoint,

164
00:09:42,945 --> 00:09:46,476
and the quiz asks
where its preprocessing should live.

165
00:09:46,796 --> 00:09:49,825
Picture two clients:
a phone app, and a web page.

166
00:09:50,066 --> 00:09:54,910
Each client carries the same gear:
decode the JPEG,

167
00:09:54,910 --> 00:10:00,797
scale the pixels to between 0 and 1,
and resize to 224 by 224.

168
00:10:01,117 --> 00:10:03,719
Only then does it send numbers to the model.

169
00:10:04,039 --> 00:10:07,560
That is two copies of one recipe,
in two code bases.

170
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:10,360
The lecture calls
this client-side brittleness.

171
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:13,660
Every client preprocesses
slightly differently,

172
00:10:13,660 --> 00:10:17,400
and a model trained
on one recipe quietly receives another.

173
00:10:17,716 --> 00:10:19,721
Now move the gear into the endpoint.

174
00:10:20,041 --> 00:10:23,714
Clients send raw JPEG bytes,
wrapped as base64,

175
00:10:23,714 --> 00:10:26,527
because JSON cannot carry raw bytes.

176
00:10:26,847 --> 00:10:29,388
The preprocessing travels with the model,

177
00:10:29,388 --> 00:10:33,354
so every client gets exactly
the preprocessing used in training.

178
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:35,413
Look at the repo's own client.

179
00:10:35,733 --> 00:10:40,299
It reads the image files,
base64 encodes them, and calls predict.

180
00:10:40,619 --> 00:10:42,965
No resizing, no normalizing.

181
00:10:43,285 --> 00:10:44,778
That is a thin client.

182
00:10:45,016 --> 00:10:47,427
Now ship a bad resize in version 2.

183
00:10:47,747 --> 00:10:49,453
Both clients break at once.

184
00:10:49,773 --> 00:10:53,827
Roll back to version 1,
and both are healthy at the same instant.

185
00:10:54,147 --> 00:10:57,539
One deployment fixed everyone,
with no client release.

186
00:10:57,783 --> 00:10:59,916
Now put the gears back on the clients.

187
00:11:00,236 --> 00:11:02,028
What does the same rollback cost?

188
00:11:08,866 --> 00:11:12,794
Two releases:
a phone update waiting in app store review,

189
00:11:12,794 --> 00:11:13,965
and a web deploy.

190
00:11:14,285 --> 00:11:18,253
They land at different times,
and in between, the clients disagree.

191
00:11:18,500 --> 00:11:21,697
The rule:
when the question says several clients,

192
00:11:21,697 --> 00:11:23,849
one place to update or roll back,

193
00:11:23,849 --> 00:11:27,567
and no client release,
put preprocessing at the endpoint.

194
00:11:27,887 --> 00:11:32,686
Preprocessing on the clients means
every client is re-released in lockstep.

195
00:11:37,633 --> 00:11:42,582
Preprocessing at the endpoint works only
if the endpoint calls the right function.

196
00:11:42,902 --> 00:11:46,699
Here is the chain:
JPEG bytes, decode, convert to floats,

197
00:11:46,699 --> 00:11:50,561
resize, the model,
and four probabilities, one per cheese.

198
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:55,280
In the repo, preprocess_image
does the first three steps, for one image.

199
00:11:55,600 --> 00:11:57,882
It returns an image, not an answer.

200
00:11:58,116 --> 00:12:00,947
The serving_function
takes a batch of strings,

201
00:12:00,947 --> 00:12:04,516
maps preprocess_image over each one,
then calls the model.

202
00:12:04,836 --> 00:12:07,631
Preprocessing and inference,
in one function.

203
00:12:07,951 --> 00:12:11,257
Its argument, bytes_inputs,
is the key clients use.

204
00:12:11,577 --> 00:12:16,609
Exam questions may call this function serve:
not the web app's serve command,

205
00:12:16,609 --> 00:12:19,876
but the function
that preprocesses, then predicts.

206
00:12:20,116 --> 00:12:23,377
The export registers it
under the name serving_default,

207
00:12:23,377 --> 00:12:26,815
and the model server calls
whatever sits behind that name.

208
00:12:27,135 --> 00:12:30,954
The slide saves it with a different call;
the idea is the same.

209
00:12:31,200 --> 00:12:33,547
Before we try plugs, one last check.

210
00:12:33,867 --> 00:12:38,346
A phone app sends float arrays,
a new web client sends JPEG bytes,

211
00:12:38,346 --> 00:12:41,739
and the team wants one place
to fix preprocessing.

212
00:12:42,059 --> 00:12:43,530
What should they register?

213
00:12:56,366 --> 00:12:59,801
Try B first:
register preprocess_image alone.

214
00:13:00,121 --> 00:13:05,241
A request comes back as a 224 by 224 image.

215
00:13:05,561 --> 00:13:07,076
Nothing was classified.

216
00:13:07,316 --> 00:13:09,065
A and D keep the raw model.

217
00:13:09,385 --> 00:13:13,311
It demands float tensors,
so JPEG bytes no longer fit.

218
00:13:13,631 --> 00:13:16,852
The server only turns
base64 back into bytes.

219
00:13:17,172 --> 00:13:21,418
The gears jump back onto every client:
two copies to keep in sync.

220
00:13:21,666 --> 00:13:25,741
C, the serving_function:
bytes go in, probabilities come out.

221
00:13:26,061 --> 00:13:29,155
The phone app switches
to sending bytes once;

222
00:13:29,155 --> 00:13:32,593
after that,
one exported version holds the recipe,

223
00:13:32,593 --> 00:13:35,618
and every fix or rollback is one deployment.

224
00:13:35,938 --> 00:13:40,405
The repo's test step loads serving_default
and feeds it bytes_inputs,

225
00:13:40,405 --> 00:13:41,634
as the server will.

226
00:13:41,883 --> 00:13:44,404
This echoes the function's entry point:

227
00:13:44,404 --> 00:13:47,701
the platform calls exactly
the name you registered,

228
00:13:47,701 --> 00:13:50,480
and nothing downstream can fix a wrong one.

229
00:13:50,716 --> 00:13:55,025
The rule: register the function
that does preprocessing and inference.

230
00:13:55,345 --> 00:13:57,775
Preprocessing alone returns images,

231
00:13:57,775 --> 00:14:01,106
and the raw model makes
every client preprocess.

232
00:14:05,550 --> 00:14:07,171
Seven rules for Tuesday.

233
00:14:07,491 --> 00:14:09,219
One: read the lifecycle.

234
00:14:09,539 --> 00:14:11,804
If it listens, it is a service;

235
00:14:11,804 --> 00:14:17,283
if it runs and exits, it is a job,
started on demand or by Cloud Scheduler.

236
00:14:17,603 --> 00:14:20,697
Two: idle time costs only what is always on.

237
00:14:21,017 --> 00:14:25,161
Three: pay-per-use bills
invocations and execution time,

238
00:14:25,161 --> 00:14:28,121
and zero idle cost buys you cold starts.

239
00:14:28,367 --> 00:14:32,283
Four: a function is source,
requirements and an entry point,

240
00:14:32,283 --> 00:14:36,004
and Google builds the image;
a service is your own image.

241
00:14:36,324 --> 00:14:39,161
Five: who may call it is not who it runs as,

242
00:14:39,161 --> 00:14:42,063
and the runtime
service account needs no key.

243
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:44,509
Six: preprocess at the endpoint,

244
00:14:44,509 --> 00:14:48,167
so one deployment
updates or rolls back every client.

245
00:14:48,487 --> 00:14:53,570
Seven: register the serving function
that preprocesses and then calls the model,

246
00:14:53,570 --> 00:14:57,191
because the platform calls
exactly the name you register.

247
00:14:57,433 --> 00:14:58,670
Which one to use?

248
00:14:58,990 --> 00:15:01,251
The next lecture's rule of thumb:

249
00:15:01,251 --> 00:15:04,608
Vertex AI to serve a trained model
in production,

250
00:15:04,608 --> 00:15:07,993
Cloud Run
when the model is part of a broader app.

251
00:15:08,313 --> 00:15:10,793
And remember the costs on slide 16:

252
00:15:10,793 --> 00:15:14,052
vendor lock-in,
cold starts, harder debugging.

253
00:15:14,300 --> 00:15:16,305
That's the lecture in seven rules.

254
00:15:16,625 --> 00:15:18,012
Good luck on Tuesday.

